ame":"bootgly/bootgly","commit_timestamp":"2026-11-01T20:52:55Z","branch":"main","commit_sha":"4c369aaeb5f4edd2844a64b5a6678b68c59bcc2e","github_link":"https://github.com/bootgly/bootgly/commit/4c369aaeb5f4edd2844a64b5a6678b68c59bcc2e","commit_subject":"fix(adi): refuse a MySQL parameter count the statement does not take","ai_tool":"Claude"},{"repo_name":"karatelabs/karate","commit_timestamp":"2026-11-01T20:52:47Z","branch":"main","commit_sha":"de349d6320690fdab236fe444131a9971d65bfc1","github_link":"https://github.com/karatelabs/karate/commit/de349d6320690fdab236fe444131a9971d65bfc1","commit_subject":"karate-core: a wildcard HttpServer on port 0 no longer takes a port that another socket holds on 127.0.0.1. On macOS a wildcard bind can sit beside a 127.0.0.1 bind on the same port, and port 0 hands out ports in sequence without skipping one held on loopback. A server started on port 0 could therefore get the port of, for example, a Gradle daemon listening on 127.0.0.1:59819. A client calling localhost then reached that other socket, which closed the connection: \"The target server failed to respond\". This made karate-ext's suite fail intermittently whenever the port allocator reached such a port (McpClientNegotiationTest, and TwinOrdersTest's order deck, where the twin's first /reset failed so the deck had no FAIL row; RunnerMutateApiTest's MaxServer too). After a wildcard bind on port 0, HttpServer now tries to bind 127.0.0.1 at the port it got, and binds again (up to 16 times) while another socket holds that port. The probe runs only where a 127.0.0.1 bind can sit beside a wildcard one, which is checked once (not on Linux, where the kernel already refuses the shared port). HttpServerBindTest advances the allocator to a port held on 127.0.0.1 and fails without the fix.","ai_tool":"Claude"},{"repo_name":"pandas-dev/pandas","commit_timestamp":"2026-11-01T20:52:44Z","branch":"main","commit_sha":"11f0a53821c39417fe1812d81e981216babcd039","github_link":"https://github.